00:05:33,913 --> 00:05:39,913
1964. When America was at war with itself.

2
00:11:07,826 --> 00:11:13,826
Mississippi Burning is a drama, crime, mystery film directed by Alan Parker.

3
00:16:41,739 --> 00:16:47,739
Two FBI agents investigating the murder of civil rights workers during the 60s seek to breach the
conspiracy of silence in a small Southern town where segregation divides black and white.

4
00:22:15,652 --> 00:22:21,652
The younger agent trained in FBI school runs up against the
small town ways of his partner, a former sheriff.

5
00:27:49,565 --> 00:27:55,565
Agent Rupert Anderson, played by Gene Hackman.

6
00:33:23,478 --> 00:33:29,478
Agent Alan Ward, played by Willem Dafoe.

7
00:38:57,391 --> 00:39:03,391
Mrs. Pell, played by Frances McDormand.

8
00:44:31,304 --> 00:44:37,304
Deputy Clinton Pell, played by Brad Dourif.

9
00:50:05,217 --> 00:50:11,217
Mayor Tilman, played by R. Lee Ermey.

10
00:55:39,130 --> 00:55:45,130
Sheriff Stuckey, played by Gailard Sartain.

11
01:01:13,043 --> 01:01:19,043
Mississippi Burning is a 1988 American crime thriller film directed by Alan Parker and written by Chris Gerolmo that is loosely
based on the 1964 investigation into the murders of Chaney, Goodman, and Schwerner in Neshoba County, Mississippi.

12
01:06:46,956 --> 01:06:52,956
It stars Gene Hackman and Willem Dafoe as two FBI agents investigating the disappearance of three civil rights activists in fictional
Jessup County, Mississippi, who are met with hostility by the town's residents, local police, and the Ku Klux Klan.

13
01:12:20,869 --> 01:12:26,869
Gerolmo began writing the script in 1986 after researching the 1964
murders of James Chaney, Andrew Goodman and Michael Schwerner.

14
01:17:54,782 --> 01:18:00,782
He and producer Frederick Zollo presented it to Orion
Pictures, and the studio hired Parker to direct.

15
01:23:28,695 --> 01:23:34,695
The writer and director had disputes over the script, and
Orion allowed Parker to make uncredited rewrites.

16
01:29:02,608 --> 01:29:08,608
The film was shot in a number of locations in Mississippi and
Alabama, with principal photography from March to May 1988.

17
01:34:36,521 --> 01:34:42,521
On release, Mississippi Burning was criticized by activists involved in the civil rights movement
and the families of Chaney, Goodman and Schwerner for its fictionalization of events.

18
01:40:10,434 --> 01:40:16,434
Critical reaction was generally positive, with praise for the cinematography
and the performances of Hackman, Dafoe and Frances McDormand.

19
01:45:44,347 --> 01:45:50,347
The film grossed $34.6 million in North America
against a production budget of $15 million.

20
01:51:18,260 --> 01:51:24,260
It received seven Academy Award nominations, including Best
Picture, and won for Best Cinematography.

21
01:56:52,173 --> 01:56:58,173
The film explores themes of suicide, sheriff, police, funeral, ku klux klan.

22
02:02:26,086 --> 02:02:32,086
The tone is intense and lighthearted and emotional.
